WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.640
Hurricane Juan in 1985. [laughing] I'll never stay again.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:12.320
That was really an experience, and it only had eighty mile-an-hour winds.

3
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:22.100
We had pecan trees in our front yard, and to see those pecan trees just bow down to that wind is an awesome sight.

4
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.620
To watch the water just to keep creeping up.

5
00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:32.340
And we didn't get water in our home, but we had about two and a half feet of water in our yard.

6
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:38.600
That's all in the . . . sort of the middle of the island because, you know, Grand Isle's like a turtle back.

7
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:42.500
It slopes to the beach, it slopes to the bay, it slopes to the east and the west.

8
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:47.980
But you have this high ridge in the middle of the island that's about five feet above sea level.

9
00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:55.020
And to have three feet of . . . almost three feet of water in the yard was really something to see.

10
00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:55.920
It was scary.

11
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.520
I wouldn't stay again.

12
00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:05.160
I don't own anything worth risking the life of my children, or myself, or anyone that I love.

13
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:10.620
Damage that it did to us, just by sitting out there and driving the tide chain . . . you know,

14
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.020
bringing in high tides to us and a lot of water.

15
00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:14.380
Yeah.

16
00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:25.120
That seems to be the big concern is that these low level tropical systems that move very slow along the coast will cause so much more erosion than a big storm, you know.

17
00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:25.360
Correct.

18
00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.100
They do.

19
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:32.380
Because they're just out there that much longer doing as much damage as they can, yeah.

20
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:33.340
They do.

21
00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:39.600
I know that if you have a slow moving winter storm, that comes out of, say,

22
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.720
the northwest, the back side of Grand Isle on the bay side just gets beaten up.

23
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:50.100
And it's the same thing in the summertime if you get a southerly storm coming in.

24
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:56.560
It doesn't have to be a tropical storm, just a weather system coming in and beats us up.

25
00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.280
So if we're getting beat up,

26
00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:03.640
I know that the rest of the barrier islands that have less protection than we do are getting the same experience.
